msgid "Day one"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:799
msgid "Keep a second browser (or a private window) logged in while you change the login address, the CAPTCHA or two-factor settings."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:800
msgid "Change the login address and confirm it. Test it in a private window."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:801
msgid "Tell other people who log in about the new address. Links inside WordPress (emails, the admin bar, “Log in” links) update on their own."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:802
msgid "If a page cache is in use, check that the login address is not cached. See the cache article."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:804
msgid "During the week"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:805
msgid "Run a CAPTCHA in test mode. The Safety panel shows how many submissions would have been blocked in the last 7 days."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:806
msgid "Look at <strong>Activity</strong>. Failed logins against usernames that do not exist are bots; failures on real accounts from one place may be a forgotten password."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:807
msgid "Look at the Leak Check result on the dashboard. “Passed” means none of the known routes reveal your login address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:809
msgid "After a week"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:810
msgid "Turn off CAPTCHA test mode if nobody real would have been blocked."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:811
msgid "Ask everyone with an administrator account to set up two-factor login."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:812
msgid "Export your settings (Authlify → Settings) and keep the file with your site backups."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:814
#, php-format
msgid "If anything goes wrong, %s lists every way back in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:814
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1680
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1732
msgid "Get back in when locked out"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:850
msgid "Login URL and hiding"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:850
msgid "Move the login page to a private address and hide wp-login.php and /wp-admin/ from visitors who are not logged in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:852
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:910
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:950
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:988
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1071
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1154
msgid "Purpose"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:852
msgid "Almost every attack on a WordPress login is a script that posts guesses to wp-login.php. When the login page lives at an address only you know, those scripts get a “page not found” and give up. It is not a replacement for strong passwords or lockouts, but it removes most of the noise."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:853
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:911
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:951
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:989
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1005
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1037
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1072
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1114
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1140
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1155
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1221
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1257
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1275
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1299
msgid "Where to find it"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:853
#, php-format
msgid "%s. The screen has two tabs: Login URL and Redirects."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:854
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:952
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:990
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1006
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1038
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1141
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1156
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1258
msgid "How to configure it"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:854
#: inc/Admin/LoginUrlPage.php:82
msgid "Your login address"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:855
msgid "<strong>New login address</strong>: 3 to 64 characters of letters, numbers, hyphens and underscores, with at least one letter. It is lowercased."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:856
msgid "Some words are reserved because WordPress uses them: for example <code>login</code>, <code>admin</code>, <code>dashboard</code>, <code>wp-admin</code>, <code>wp-login</code>, <code>register</code>, <code>feed</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>author</code>, <code>sitemap</code> and WordPress query words such as <code>p</code> or <code>s</code>. The address also cannot match an existing page or post."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:857
msgid "Leave the field empty to go back to the standard wp-login.php page. That applies immediately."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:858
#: inc/Admin/LoginUrlPage.php:106
msgid "Hide the default login"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:859
msgid "<strong>Hide default URLs</strong> (on by default) hides wp-login.php and wp-admin from visitors. It needs a custom login address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:860
msgid "<strong>Show visitors</strong>: your theme’s “page not found” page (recommended), an “access denied” message, or a redirect."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:861
msgid "<strong>Redirect to</strong>: used with the redirect option. Use an address on this site; leave it empty for the homepage."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:863
msgid "How to use it"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:864
msgid "After you save a new address, click <strong>Open and confirm the new URL</strong> in the notice. The change only takes effect once you open that link while logged in as an administrator."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:865
msgid "Until then both the old and the new address work. After 30 minutes an unconfirmed change expires and nothing changes. <strong>Cancel the change</strong> ends it early."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:866
msgid "Once confirmed, the new address is emailed to the site admin address and to you."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:868
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:968
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:994
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1042
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1083
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1147
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1233
msgid "What to expect"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:869
msgid "The login page answers at <code>https://example.com/your-address/</code> (and at <code>?your-address</code>). It is never cached and tells search engines not to index it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:870
msgid "Visitors who are not logged in get the chosen response for wp-login.php (including disguised variants such as <code>//wp-login.php</code> or URL-encoded names), wp-register.php, and any page under /wp-admin/."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:871
msgid "Inside /wp-admin/, the “page not found” response is a redirect to <code>/404/</code> on your site."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:872
msgid "The WordPress shortcuts <code>/login</code>, <code>/admin</code> and <code>/dashboard</code> stop working for visitors who are not logged in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:873
msgid "WordPress links to wp-login.php (the lost-password link, “Log in” links, emails) point to your new address automatically."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:874
msgid "admin-ajax.php and admin-post.php stay reachable, so front-end forms keep working. Password-protected posts keep working too."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:875
msgid "Logged-in people opening the login address go straight to the dashboard (or the redirect you set). wp-login.php itself shows the blocked response to everyone, logged in or not, so always use your own address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:877
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:929
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:977
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:995
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1014
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1047
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1120
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1174
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1196
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1268
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1292
msgid "Limitations"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:878
msgid "Hiding the login page is not a security boundary on its own. Keep brute-force lockouts on."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:879
msgid "Logged-in members who cannot edit content (subscribers, customers) get a log-out link without the login address, are not shown it on wp-admin screens, and land on the home page after logging out. Their session-expired popup is off too. Anyone who logs in on the login page, or opens a password-reset link from an email, still sees the address: the reset screen lives there. “Lost your password?” links on public pages, such as Easy Digital Downloads’ login form, and unlock emails asked for on a public form use small screens on the home page that do not name the address. A front-end form built with <code>wp_login_form()</code> still posts to it, and Leak Check warns about those pages. Staff keep the normal link and popup. The <code>authlify_hide_login_from_member</code> filter changes who counts."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:880
msgid "On a private site (Force login), visitors are sent to your login address, so it is no longer secret."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:881
msgid "WooCommerce’s My Account login form and other front-end login forms are not hidden; they are protected by lockouts and CAPTCHA instead."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:882
msgid "On multisite, wp-signup.php and wp-activate.php are not hidden because the network needs them."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:883
msgid "Do not run a second “hide login” plugin at the same time. Authlify warns you if it finds one."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:885
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1180
#, php-format
msgid "Related: %1$s, %2$s, %3$s."
msgstr ""

