msgid "CAPTCHA"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:903
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1180
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1204
msgid "Two-factor recovery"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:908
msgid "An automatic test that visits your site as a stranger would and proves the hidden login address is not revealed anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:910
msgid "Many “hide login” plugins can be found in one request: a password-reset link, a redirect from /wp-admin/ or a theme link gives the secret address away. Leak Check tries those routes against your own site and reports what it finds."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:911
#, php-format
msgid "The Leak Check panel on the %1$s, a status line on the %2$s screen, a dashboard checklist item, WordPress Site Health, and the <code>wp authlify leak-check</code> command."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:911
#: inc/Admin/Menu.php:30
msgid "Dashboard"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:911
#: inc/Admin/LoginUrlPage.php:46
#: inc/Admin/Menu.php:31
#: inc/Admin/Menu.php:90
#: inc/Admin/ProPage.php:122
msgid "Login URL"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:912
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1115
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:1591
msgid "How it works"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:913
msgid "It only sends requests to your own site’s address, logged out, without following redirects and without ever submitting a password. Each request carries a signed header, so the probes are not written to the activity log."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:914
msgid "It checks that the login address works and is not cached, then tries 50 or more routes: wp-login.php and disguised variants, every login action (register, lost password, logout and others), /wp-admin/ pages, the /login, /admin and /dashboard shortcuts, old Modify Login back doors, and public pages such as the homepage, a post, a 404 page, robots.txt, the sitemap, the feed and the REST index."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:915
msgid "A route leaks if it shows the login form, or mentions the address in a redirect or in the page."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:916
msgid "It also visits front-end login pages: /login/, /my-account/, /account/, /members/ and /register/, the login, account and registration pages of WooCommerce, Easy Digital Downloads, Paid Memberships Pro, Ultimate Member and BuddyPress, the WooCommerce cart and checkout, and pages with a login form or an Authlify block. A login form on a public page has to send people to your login address, so anyone who opens that page can read it: this is reported as a warning, not a leak."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:917
msgid "It also reports, as warnings, things that help attackers without revealing the address: XML-RPC multi-password requests, usernames in the REST API, and <code>?author=</code> scans."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:919
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1145
msgid "When it runs"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:920
msgid "About ten seconds after the login address or hiding settings change."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:921
msgid "Once a week, on the daily maintenance task."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:922
msgid "When you click <strong>Run Leak Check</strong> (at most once every 30 seconds), or run the WP-CLI command."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:923
msgid "It does nothing while no custom login address is set."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:925
msgid "Reading the result"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:925
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:1621
msgid "Passed"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:925
msgid "No route revealed the login address. Warnings about XML-RPC, REST usernames or author scans can still be listed; fix them on the Hardening tab."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:926
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:1618
msgid "Warning"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:926
msgid "No hidden route leaked, but a public login page (a front-end login form) shows the address, or wp-login.php is not hidden. Keep the page if you want it: brute-force limits, CAPTCHA and two-factor login still protect the login. A result older than 14 days is also marked out of date in Site Health."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:927
#: templates/admin/logs.php:163
#: templates/admin/logs.php:235
msgid "Failed"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:927
msgid "At least one route revealed the address. Each result names the route and how to fix it. A theme or another plugin that prints wp_login_url() on public pages is the usual cause."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:928
msgid "Incomplete or untested"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:928
msgid "Your server could not reach itself (a “loopback” request). Some hosts block this; Site Health reports the same problem."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:929
msgid "Leak Check tests known routes on your own site. It cannot see links to your login address posted elsewhere, and it does not test pages behind a login."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:944
msgid "Authlify → Security → Brute force"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:945
msgid "Authlify → Security → Hardening"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:948
msgid "Lock out addresses that keep guessing passwords, with lockouts that grow longer for repeat offenders."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:950
msgid "A password-guessing script tries thousands of passwords. Limiting failed attempts per IP address makes that impossible, while everyone else keeps logging in as normal."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:953
msgid "<strong>Brute-force protection</strong>: on by default on new sites."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:954
msgid "<strong>Failed attempts allowed</strong> (default 5) within the <strong>Time window</strong> (default 15 minutes), per IP address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:955
msgid "<strong>Lockout length</strong> (default 15 minutes)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:956
msgid "<strong>Escalating lockouts</strong> (on by default): repeat lockouts last 1×, 4×, 16× and then 96× the lockout length. With the default 15 minutes that is 15 minutes, 1 hour, 4 hours, then 24 hours. The count starts again once an address has had no lockout for 24 hours."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:957
msgid "<strong>Network lockouts</strong> (off by default): when addresses from one network (an IPv4 /24 or IPv6 /48) fail three times the allowed attempts in total, the whole network is locked. It stops attackers who rotate addresses, but it can also lock out an office or a mobile carrier, so add your own address to “Never lock out” first."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:958
msgid "<strong>Targeted-account threshold</strong> (default 10): when one username collects this many failures from different addresses, the account is not locked out. Instead, if a CAPTCHA is set to “Only after failed logins”, the login form asks for it for that username."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:959
msgid "<strong>Account pause</strong> (on by default): at twice the targeted-account threshold, the account is paused for addresses it has never logged in from, for the lockout length. The owner still logs in from any address they used before or on “Never lock out”, and the lockout message offers an unlock email."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:960
msgid "An IPv6 visitor is counted by their /64, because one connection usually owns a whole /64."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:962
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1548
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:124
msgid "Refused usernames"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:963
msgid "Off until you add names, such as admin, administrator or test. Nobody can register a listed name on any form that creates users through WordPress. An administrator or WP-CLI can still create one on purpose."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:964
msgid "A name that belongs to a real account is always skipped, and the screen names it, so listing one never affects that person. Email addresses never match."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:965
msgid "<strong>Login attempts</strong> (off by default): attempts with a listed name are refused before anything else runs and logged as “Refused username”. The visitor sees an ordinary error."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:966
msgid "<strong>Immediate lockout</strong> (off by default): the first such attempt locks the address, for the normal lockout length. It never applies to private addresses (a proxy that is not set up makes every visitor look like one), an address an administrator has logged in from, or the “Never lock out” list. Registration attempts never lock anyone out, because a real visitor can pick a listed name by chance."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:969
msgid "When one or two attempts remain, the error says so. (This hint is hidden when “Login error messages” is on.)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:970
msgid "A locked-out visitor sees “Too many failed login attempts. Please try again in N minutes”, with a link to email themselves an unlock link."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:971
msgid "The lock applies to the address, for every account, administrators included. People already logged in are not affected."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:972
msgid "Logins through XML-RPC, the REST API and application passwords are counted and locked too."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:973
msgid "A successful login clears that address’s failure count, but not an active lockout."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:974
msgid "Current lockouts are listed under <strong>Locked out right now</strong>, with <strong>Unlock</strong> and <strong>Unlock everyone</strong>. Unlocking an address also clears its network."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:975
msgid "Old counters are cleaned up daily."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:978
msgid "Lockouts only work if Authlify sees each visitor’s real IP address. Behind Cloudflare or a proxy, set the visitor IP option first."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:979
msgid "The “Never lock out” list matters only while brute-force protection is on. The “Always block” list applies either way."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:980
msgid "On multisite the counters and lockouts are shared by the whole network."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:982
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1090
#, php-format
msgid "Related: %1$s, %2$s."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:982
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:986
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:99
msgid "Visitor IP address"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:982
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1003
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:118
msgid "Allow and block lists"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:986
msgid "Tell Authlify how visitors reach your site, so lockouts hit the attacker and not your CDN."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:988
msgid "Behind Cloudflare, a load balancer or a reverse proxy, every request seems to come from the proxy. If Authlify locked that address, it would lock out everyone. Forwarding headers carry the real address, but anyone can fake them, so Authlify only reads them from proxies it can trust."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:989
#, php-format
msgid "%s, panel <strong>Visitor IP address</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:990
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:100
msgid "Detected setup"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:990
msgid "A suggestion based on your own current request, with “Matches your setting” or “Recommended: change the setting below”, and the IP address Authlify sees for you right now. It never changes the setting by itself."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:991
msgid "Directly (default)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:991
msgid "Uses the connecting address. Right for sites with no proxy or CDN in front."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:992
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:112
msgid "Through Cloudflare"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:992
msgid "Uses the <code>CF-Connecting-IP</code> header, but only when the request really comes from one of Cloudflare’s published address ranges."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:993
msgid "Through my own proxy or load balancer"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:993
msgid "Uses <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, but only when the request comes from an address in <strong>Trusted proxies</strong> (one IP or CIDR range per line). It reads the header from the right and takes the first address that is not one of your proxies."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:994
msgid "After saving, “Your IP address as seen now” should show your real public address, not your host’s or Cloudflare’s. WordPress Site Health also warns when the setting and your setup disagree."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:996
msgid "The Cloudflare address ranges are built into the plugin. Cloudflare rarely changes them, but a request from a brand-new range would be treated as direct."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:997
msgid "The <code>X-Real-IP</code> header is not used."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1003
msgid "Addresses that are never locked out, and addresses that can never log in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1005
#, php-format
msgid "%s, panel <strong>Allow and block lists</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1007
msgid "<strong>Never lock out</strong>: your office or home IP. Failures from these addresses are not counted, they are never locked out, and they never see a CAPTCHA."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1008
msgid "<strong>Always block</strong>: these addresses can never log in. They get “Access from your network is blocked.” They can still view the site."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1009
msgid "One IPv4 or IPv6 address or CIDR range per line (commas also work), for example <code>203.0.113.7</code>, <code>198.51.100.0/24</code> or <code>2001:db8::/32</code>. Invalid entries are refused when you save."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1010
msgid "You cannot save a block list that contains your own current address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1011
msgid "<strong>Block in one click</strong>: on Authlify → Activity, each address has a <strong>Block</strong> link, and each row of <strong>Locked out right now</strong> has a <strong>Block</strong> button (<strong>Block network</strong> for a network lockout). It adds the address to Always block after you confirm, and is recorded in the activity log as “Added to block list”. It is not offered for your own current address, private or local addresses, addresses already on either list, or when the log stores anonymized addresses."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1012
msgid "<strong>Block repeat offenders</strong> (0, off, by default): after this many lockouts of one address in a row, the address is added to Always block. A day without a lockout starts the count again. Addresses an administrator has logged in from, and private or local addresses, are never added; such skips are logged as “Automatic block skipped”. It stops adding once the list holds 2,000 lines."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1014
msgid "Both lists apply even when lockouts are switched off. They use the address from the Visitor IP setting, so set that first. Blocking is permanent until you remove the line from Always block."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1019
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:48
msgid "Hardening"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1019
msgid "Close the other ways into WordPress: XML-RPC, application passwords, username discovery and revealing error messages."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1020
#, php-format
msgid "Where: %s. Every option here starts at the WordPress default, which leaves things as they are, until you change it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1022
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1551
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:218
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:402
#: inc/Diagnostics/SiteHealth.php:603
msgid "XML-RPC"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1023
msgid "<strong>On</strong> (default): unchanged. Some apps and Jetpack need it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1024
msgid "<strong>Block multi-password requests</strong>: removes <code>system.multicall</code>, which lets one request try hundreds of passwords. Such requests get an error (HTTP 403). Everything else keeps working."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1025
msgid "<strong>Off</strong>: every request to xmlrpc.php gets HTTP 403, and the pingback header and discovery link are removed. Choose it if you do not use the WordPress mobile app, Jetpack or remote publishing."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1027
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1552
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:223
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:406
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:976
#: inc/Diagnostics/SiteHealth.php:604
msgid "Application passwords"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1027
msgid "They let apps log in through the REST API without the login page, CAPTCHA or two-factor. Choose <strong>All users</strong> (default), <strong>Administrators only</strong> (on multisite: super admins only) or <strong>Off</strong>. Brute-force limits apply to them either way."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1028
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1553
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:231
msgid "Username discovery"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1028
msgid "When on, visitors who are not logged in can no longer list usernames: the REST API users endpoints disappear, <code>?author=</code> links and author archives return “page not found”, the users sitemap is removed and oEmbed data no longer names the author. Logged-in people still see author archives."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1029
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1554
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:232
msgid "Login error messages"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1029
msgid "When on, a failed login always says “The username or password is incorrect.” instead of telling an attacker which one was wrong."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1030
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:235
msgid "Private site"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1030
#, php-format
msgid "See %s."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1030
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1035
msgid "Force login (private site)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1035
msgid "Only logged-in users can see the site; everyone else is sent to the login page."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1037
#, php-format
msgid "%s, panel <strong>Private site</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1039
msgid "<strong>Force login</strong>: “Require login to view the site” (off by default)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1040
msgid "<strong>Public pages</strong>: paths that stay public, one per line (commas also work). A path includes its sub-pages: <code>/shop</code> covers <code>/shop/</code> and <code>/shop/item</code>, but not <code>/shopping</code>. Use <code>/</code> for the home page only. Full URLs are accepted; only the path is used. There are no wildcards."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1043
msgid "Visitors who are not logged in are redirected to your login address, and come back to the page they wanted after logging in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1044
msgid "Anonymous REST API requests get a “You must be logged in to use this site.” error (HTTP 401)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1045
msgid "The login page itself, robots.txt, admin-ajax and scheduled tasks keep working. RSS feeds are redirected too."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1048
msgid "Because every visitor is sent to the login page, the custom login address is no longer secret on a private site. Leak Check reports those redirects as \"Info\", not as leaks."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1049
msgid "XML-RPC is not affected. Set it to Off on the same tab if you do not need it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1050
msgid "Pages that other services must reach (payment callbacks, webhooks, a WooCommerce shop) need a Public pages entry."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1066
msgid "Authlify → Security → CAPTCHA"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1069
msgid "CAPTCHA, modes and safety"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1069
msgid "Stop scripts at the form, choose when people see the check, and decide what happens if the provider is down."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1071
msgid "A CAPTCHA stops scripts that guess passwords, create fake accounts or post spam before they reach WordPress. Lockouts react after failures; a CAPTCHA stops many attempts from counting at all."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1072
#, php-format
msgid "%s, with three panels: CAPTCHA provider, Where and when, and Safety."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1073
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1567
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:191
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:192
msgid "Forms"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1073
msgid "Login, Registration and Lost password are ticked by default once a provider is chosen. You can also protect comments from visitors who are not logged in, and, when WooCommerce is active, its login, registration, lost password and checkout forms. The checkout switch covers guest orders on both the classic checkout and the Checkout block; with only WooCommerce registration ticked, the Checkout block asks for the CAPTCHA only when the order creates an account."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1074
msgid "Other plugins’ forms follow the same switches while the plugin is active: the login forms of Easy Digital Downloads, Ultimate Member and MemberPress follow <strong>Login</strong>; the sign-up forms of Easy Digital Downloads, Ultimate Member and BuddyPress or BuddyBoss, Paid Memberships Pro’s checkout for people without an account, and LearnDash’s registration form follow <strong>Registration</strong>; the lost-password forms of Easy Digital Downloads (block), Ultimate Member and Paid Memberships Pro follow <strong>Lost password</strong>. Login forms built with WordPress’s own form, as in Paid Memberships Pro and LearnDash, follow <strong>Login</strong> without any extra step. LearnDash’s reset-password form is not covered, because LearnDash offers no documented way to refuse that request. The panel lists the plugins it found. MemberPress, BuddyBoss and LearnDash support is in beta: it is built on their documented hooks and has not been tested against those plugins themselves."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1075
msgid "On every login form the CAPTCHA is checked before the password, so a bot that leaves it out is refused without a password check. Lockouts, the block list and two-factor login apply to every login that goes through WordPress, whichever form it comes from; Ultimate Member and Easy Digital Downloads show the lockout message instead of their own “wrong password” text."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1075
msgid "Logins through XML-RPC, the REST API and application passwords cannot show a CAPTCHA; the brute-force limits cover those."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1076
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1568
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:226
msgid "When to show it"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1076
msgid "Always (default)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1076
msgid "Every protected form shows the check."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1077
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:228
msgid "Only after failed logins"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1077
msgid "The WordPress and WooCommerce login forms show it only after “Failed logins before it appears” (default 2) failures from the visitor’s address, or when the username is under attack from many addresses (the targeted-account threshold on the Brute force tab). Other forms always show it. Honest visitors who type their password correctly never see it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1078
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:241
msgid "Safety"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1079
msgid "<strong>Test mode</strong> (off by default): the widget is shown and every answer is checked, but failures are only logged; nobody is blocked. This also applies to the honeypot. The panel then counts how many submissions would have been blocked in the last 7 days (this needs the activity log to be on)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1080
msgid "<strong>If the provider is down</strong>: “Let people through” (default; the brute-force limits still apply) or “Block the form until the provider is back”. A provider counts as down when it cannot be reached within 8 seconds or returns a server error. Outages are recorded in the activity log."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1081
msgid "<strong>Honeypot</strong>: see the honeypot article."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1084
msgid "The provider’s script loads only on pages that show a protected form."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1085
msgid "A failed check shows an error on the form and is logged as “CAPTCHA failed” with the reason. Failed CAPTCHA checks do not count towards lockouts."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1086
msgid "Addresses on “Never lock out” never see a CAPTCHA or the honeypot. Logged-in people never see one on comments or checkout."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1087
msgid "The dashboard counts CAPTCHA as done when a provider is set up for the login form and test mode is off."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1089
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1214
msgid "Emergency switch"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1089
msgid "If a CAPTCHA ever stops you logging in, add this to wp-config.php, log in and fix the settings, then remove it:"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1090
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1094
msgid "CAPTCHA providers"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1090
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1704
msgid "Add a CAPTCHA without blocking real people"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1094
msgid "Cloudflare Turnstile, ALTCHA, hCaptcha and Google reCAPTCHA v2 and v3: what each needs and what it sends where."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1096
msgid "Choosing"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1096
msgid "Turnstile suits most sites. Choose ALTCHA if nothing may be sent to another company. You can switch at any time."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1097
msgid "Cloudflare Turnstile (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1097
msgid "Free and usually shows no puzzle at all. It does not need Cloudflare hosting. Create a widget under Cloudflare dashboard → Turnstile and paste its site key and secret key. The visitor’s browser loads the widget from challenges.cloudflare.com, and your server confirms each answer there."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1098
msgid "ALTCHA (no third party)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1098
msgid "Runs entirely on your site: no keys, no cookies and nothing sent elsewhere. The browser solves a small proof-of-work puzzle in the background, and your server checks it; each answer works once. It needs JavaScript. With no third party, there is no provider to go down. To stop scripts collecting puzzles, one address that fetches more than 120 in 10 minutes gets a harder puzzle that takes a few seconds, and at ten times that it gets none until the 10 minutes pass. Change the limit with the <code>authlify_altcha_rate_limit</code> filter."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1099
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:136
#: inc/Captcha/HCaptcha.php:32
msgid "hCaptcha"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1099
msgid "Privacy-focused with a free plan; it shows image puzzles more often than Turnstile. Keys come from the hCaptcha dashboard → Sites. The widget loads from js.hcaptcha.com and answers are checked at api.hcaptcha.com."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1100
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:137
#: inc/Captcha/RecaptchaV2.php:32
msgid "Google reCAPTCHA v2"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1100
msgid "The “I’m not a robot” checkbox. Keys come from the Google reCAPTCHA admin console; pick the v2 checkbox type. It sends visitor data to Google, and free-tier limits apply."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1101
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:138
#: inc/Captcha/RecaptchaV3.php:35
msgid "Google reCAPTCHA v3"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1101
msgid "Invisible: Google scores each visit from 0.0 (bot) to 1.0 (human), and visits below the <strong>Minimum score</strong> (default 0.5) are refused. It can quietly block real people, with no puzzle to prove otherwise. v2 keys do not work with v3."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1102
msgid "Saving keys safely"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1103
msgid "Paste both keys, click <strong>Show preview</strong> and complete the check. The provider’s script loads only when you click."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1104
msgid "When you save, Authlify checks the secret key with the provider using that preview answer. A wrong key is refused and nothing is saved, so a typo cannot lock anyone out."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1105
msgid "Saving without completing the preview is refused, unless test mode is on (then it saves with a warning). If the provider cannot be reached, the keys are saved with a warning."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1106
msgid "Answers solved on another website are refused: the provider must confirm your site’s own domain."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1112
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1572
#: inc/Captcha/Admin.php:255
msgid "Honeypot"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1112
msgid "An invisible trap that catches simple bots without asking people anything."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1114
#, php-format
msgid "%s, panel <strong>Safety</strong>, <strong>Honeypot</strong> (off by default)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1116
msgid "It adds a hidden field to the same forms the CAPTCHA protects. People, screen readers and password managers never see it; bots that fill in every field do. A form sent within about 2 seconds is also refused, which can catch password-manager autofill followed by an instant click: the message says so and the next try works."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1117
msgid "It also adds a signed time stamp. A form sent back in under 2 seconds, with a missing or altered stamp, with a stamp older than a day, or with a stamp already used, is refused."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1118
msgid "It works with or without a CAPTCHA provider."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1120
msgid "A honeypot stops simple scripts, not determined attackers. Use it together with lockouts, and a CAPTCHA where spam is a problem."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1135
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1643
msgid "Authlify → Two-factor"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1138
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1582
#: inc/Security/Passwords.php:449
msgid "Breached-password check"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1138
msgid "Refuse new passwords that appear in known data breaches, without ever sending the password anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1140
#, php-format
msgid "%s, panel <strong>Breached passwords</strong>. Off by default."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1140
msgid "Authlify → Security → Passwords"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1142
msgid "<strong>Breached-password check</strong>: “Refuse passwords found in known data breaches”."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1143
msgid "<strong>Roles</strong>: tick the roles to check. Leave all unticked to check everyone."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1145
msgid "Whenever a password is set: on profile screens (your own, editing a user, adding a user), password resets, registration forms that ask for a password, and WooCommerce registration, account details and checkout account creation. It does not run at login, so existing passwords keep working."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1146
msgid "How the password stays private"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1146
msgid "Authlify hashes the password with SHA-1 and sends only the first 5 characters of that hash to the Have I Been Pwned “range” service (api.pwnedpasswords.com). The service answers with every breached hash starting with those characters, padded with decoys, and Authlify compares the rest on your server. Neither the password nor its full hash leaves your site. Answers are cached for a day."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1147
msgid "A breached password is refused with “This password has appeared N times in known data breaches, so attackers try it early. Please choose a different password.” and logged as “Breached password refused”. If the service cannot be reached within 3 seconds, the password is accepted."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1152
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1579
#: inc/Admin/ProPage.php:143
#: inc/Admin/UI.php:451
#: inc/Admin/UI.php:452
#: inc/Diagnostics/SiteHealth.php:607
#: inc/TwoFactor/AdminPage.php:81
#: inc/TwoFactor/AdminPage.php:116
#: inc/TwoFactor/LoginFlow.php:156
#: inc/TwoFactor/LoginFlow.php:702
#: inc/TwoFactor/Profile.php:227
msgid "Two-factor login"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1152
msgid "A second step after the password: an authenticator app, a passkey or a backup code."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1154
msgid "A stolen or guessed password is not enough to get in when the account also needs a code from the owner’s phone or a passkey on their device."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1155
#, php-format
msgid "Settings: %s. Each person sets it up under <strong>Users → Profile → Two-factor login</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1157
msgid "<strong>Two-factor login</strong> (on by default): lets people turn it on. When off, nobody is asked for a second step and the profile section is hidden; existing setups are kept."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1158
msgid "<strong>Offered methods</strong>: Authenticator app, Backup codes, Passkeys and security keys. Turning a method off stops new setups; people who already use it keep it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1159
msgid "<strong>Passkey sign-in</strong>: see the passkeys article."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1161
msgid "Setting it up (each person)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1162
msgid "<strong>Authenticator app</strong>: click “Set up an authenticator app”, scan the QR code with Google Authenticator, 1Password, Authy or a similar app, enter the 6-digit code and click “Verify and turn on”."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1163
msgid "<strong>Backup codes</strong>: 10 one-time codes are created automatically after the first method. Save them: they are shown once. Each works once; you are warned when two or fewer are left, and “Create new codes” replaces the whole set."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1164
msgid "<strong>Passkeys</strong>: click “Add a passkey” and follow the browser’s prompt."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1166
msgid "Signing in"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1167
msgid "After the correct password, the “Confirm it’s you” step asks for the code (or the passkey). “Having trouble?” switches to another method, such as a backup code."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1168
msgid "Codes from an authenticator app work once; a code that was just used is refused. After 5 wrong codes the person has to sign in again, and wrong codes count towards brute-force lockouts."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1169
msgid "“Remember me” from the password step is kept. The free plugin has no “trust this device” option."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1170
msgid "Logins from the WooCommerce My Account form and other front-end forms continue to the same step."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1171
msgid "Apps that log in with the account password over XML-RPC or the REST API are refused for people with two-factor; they must use an application password."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1173
msgid "Who uses it"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1173
msgid "The Two-factor screen lists, per role, how many people use two-factor (backup codes alone do not count), and the Users list gets a “2FA” column. The dashboard checklist shows how many administrators use it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1175
msgid "In the free plugin two-factor is opt-in; it cannot be required for a role."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1176
msgid "People set it up in their WordPress profile. Customers who cannot open wp-admin, for example when WooCommerce or Ultimate Member sends them away from it, cannot set it up in the free plugin. Setting it up in My Account is an Authlify Pro feature."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1177
msgid "If another two-factor plugin is active (Two Factor, WP 2FA or Wordfence Login Security), Authlify steps aside and says so."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1178
msgid "Authenticator secrets are encrypted with keys derived from your wp-config.php security keys. Changing <code>AUTH_KEY</code> or <code>SECURE_AUTH_SALT</code> makes them unreadable, so people then need a backup code, a passkey or a reset."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1180
#: inc/TwoFactor/Profile.php:152
#: inc/TwoFactor/TwoFactor.php:300
#: inc/TwoFactor/TwoFactor.php:340
msgid "Passkeys"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1180
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1640
msgid "Require two-factor for administrators"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1184
#: inc/TwoFactor/AdminPage.php:124
msgid "Passkeys and security keys"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1184
msgid "Sign in with a fingerprint, face, screen lock or hardware key, as a second step or instead of the password."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1187
msgid "PHP 8.0 or newer on the server, with the OpenSSL extension. Otherwise the option is greyed out on the Two-factor screen, with the reason shown."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1188
msgid "https (or localhost while developing), and a browser or device that supports passkeys."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1190
msgid "Setting one up"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1190
msgid "Under <strong>Users → Profile → Two-factor login</strong>, type a name such as “MacBook Touch ID”, click <strong>Add a passkey</strong> and confirm with the device. Passkeys can be renamed or removed there."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1191
msgid "Using it"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1192
msgid "<strong>As the second step</strong>: after the password, choose “Use my passkey”."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1193
msgid "<strong>Instead of the password</strong>: with <strong>Passkey sign-in</strong> on (the default), the login form shows “Sign in with a passkey” once anyone on the site has added one, in browsers that support passkeys. It counts as both factors and needs the device’s PIN or biometric."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1194
msgid "Passkey sign-in respects lockouts; failures are logged but do not count as wrong passwords."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1197
msgid "A passkey belongs to the site’s domain. After moving the site to another domain, everyone must add their passkeys again."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1198
msgid "Passkeys are stored as public keys only; the private key never leaves the person’s device."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1204
msgid "What to do when someone loses their phone and backup codes."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1206
msgid "Recovery email (self-service)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1207
msgid "On the two-factor step, “Can’t use your methods? Email me a recovery link” sends a link to the account’s email address. It is also available from a separate “Account recovery” form, which asks for the username or email and the password."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1208
msgid "The link works once, for 15 minutes, and replaces any earlier link. At most 3 links per account per hour (and 5 requests per address per hour from the separate form)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1209
msgid "Opening it shows a confirmation page first, so email scanners cannot use it. It signs the person in once and takes them to their profile to set up a new method. Their existing methods are kept, and the site admin address is notified."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1210
msgid "It is only offered after a correct password and when the account has a valid email address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1212
msgid "Reset by an administrator"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1212
#, php-format
msgid "%s → <strong>Reset for a user</strong>: find the person by username or email and click <strong>Reset</strong>. It removes their authenticator app, backup codes and passkeys (you never see their secrets), so they can sign in with their password and set up again. Administrators can also use “Reset two-factor login” on the person’s profile."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1213
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:1502
msgid "WP-CLI"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1214
msgid "This turns two-factor off for everyone without deleting anything. Remove it as soon as you are back in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1219
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1700
msgid "Login page designer"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1219
msgid "Style every login screen to match your site, with templates, “Match my site” and a live preview."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1221
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1695
msgid "Authlify → Designer"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1222
msgid "Templates and “Match my site”"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1223
msgid "Twelve templates: Default, Minimal light, Minimal dark, Glass over photo, Split image left, Split image right, Corporate blue, Soft gradient, Midnight, Warm sand, High contrast (WCAG AAA) and Sidebar."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1224
msgid "<strong>Match my site</strong> builds a design from your theme’s colors, font and logo (from theme.json and the Site Editor, the Customizer, and popular themes such as Astra, GeneratePress, Kadence and Blocksy). It reads your own site only, and nothing is saved until you click Save."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1226
#: inc/Admin/UI.php:589
#: inc/Admin/UI.php:690
msgid "Sections"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1226
msgid "Colors, Layout (centered card, split image, sidebar, full bleed or the classic WordPress placement), Background, Logo, Form, Fields, Button, Links & text (font, text size, a message above the form, footer text), Messages and Custom CSS. Fonts are system fonts, bundled fonts (Inter, Nunito, Space Grotesk, Lora) or your theme’s font; nothing is loaded from a font CDN. A warning appears when text and background colors have too little contrast."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1227
msgid "Every screen"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1227
msgid "The preview switches between Log in, Lost password, Register, Reset password, the two-factor step, the lockout message, Confirm admin email, the log-out confirmation and the “session expired” pop-up, on desktop, tablet and phone sizes. One design styles them all."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1228
msgid "Saving and undo"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1229
msgid "<strong>Use this design</strong> switches your design on or off; when off, the standard WordPress login look is used. <strong>Save</strong> (or Ctrl/Cmd+S) publishes."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1230
msgid "Undo and Redo (Ctrl/Cmd+Z) keep 100 steps. Unsaved work is kept as a draft for a day and offered back when you return."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1231
msgid "<strong>More actions</strong>: revert to saved, reset to the Default template, start from plain WordPress, export the design as JSON, or import one."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1234
msgid "Nothing changes on a new site until you switch a design on and save."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1235
msgid "The styles are compiled into a small CSS file in <code>wp-content/uploads/authlify/</code>, or printed inline if that folder is not writable."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1236
msgid "Custom CSS is limited to 20,000 characters, and anything that could run scripts or load remote styles is removed."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1237
msgid "Sites upgraded from Modify Login 2.x keep their old look. Designs from LoginPress and Colorlib Login Customizer can be imported under Authlify → Settings; imported designs go live immediately."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1255
#: inc/Admin/Menu.php:33
#: inc/Admin/Menu.php:91
#: inc/Admin/RedirectsPage.php:28
msgid "Redirects"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1255
msgid "Choose where people land after they log in or out, for everyone or by role."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1257
#, php-format
msgid "%s (a tab of the Login URL screen)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1257
msgid "Authlify → Login URL → Redirects"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1259
msgid "<strong>Everyone</strong>: <em>After login</em> and <em>After logout</em>. Leave empty for the WordPress default (the dashboard, or the page the person came from)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1260
msgid "<strong>By role</strong>: open a role to give it its own addresses. Empty fields use the addresses for everyone."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1261
msgid "Use <code>{username}</code> or <code>{user_id}</code> in an address, for example <code>/members/{username}/</code>. <code>{username}</code> is the person’s URL-friendly name (their “nicename”), not necessarily the name they log in with."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1263
msgid "Which address wins"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1264
msgid "After login: a specific destination in the login link (<code>redirect_to</code>) wins, unless it is just the dashboard. Then the first of the person’s roles that has a rule, then the address for everyone."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1265
msgid "After logout: a specific destination in the logout link always wins, then the role rule, then the address for everyone."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1266
msgid "Someone who cannot open the dashboard at all is sent to the homepage instead of a wp-admin address. A rule that points at a wp-admin screen their role cannot open still sends them there, and WordPress refuses it, so choose addresses each role can reach."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1268
msgid "Only addresses on this site, or on hosts your site allows for redirects, are used. Anything else falls back to the WordPress default."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1273
msgid "Every login, failed attempt, lockout and security change, stored only in your own database."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1275
#, php-format
msgid "%s, with a <strong>Log</strong> tab and a <strong>Settings</strong> tab. The dashboard shows the last eight entries and seven-day totals."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1275
msgid "Authlify → Activity"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1276
msgid "What is recorded"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1276
msgid "Logins, failed logins, lockouts and unlocks, blocked addresses, CAPTCHA failures, logouts, password resets, login address changes, log clearing, two-factor and passkey events, recovery emails, and refused breached passwords. Each entry keeps the time, event, user, username, IP address, browser (user agent), country when known, and details such as the channel (login form, XML-RPC, REST API or WooCommerce) and the reason for a failure."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1277
msgid "Using the log"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1278
msgid "Filter by event, by date range, or search for a username or IP address. Clicking an IP address searches for it (a partial match, so 1.2.3.4 also finds 1.2.3.45)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1279
msgid "<strong>Export CSV</strong> downloads the filtered entries (up to 100,000 rows). Cells that could be read as spreadsheet formulas are neutralised."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1280
msgid "50 entries per page, with Newer and Older buttons."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1281
msgid "<strong>Block</strong> next to an address adds it to the block list after you confirm (see Allow and block lists)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1284
msgid "<strong>Activity log</strong> (on by default). Lockouts still work when it is off. Login address changes are always recorded."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1285
msgid "<strong>Keep entries for</strong> 90 days by default; older entries are deleted daily. 0 keeps them forever."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1286
msgid "<strong>Anonymize IPs</strong> (off by default): stores IPv4 addresses without their last part (203.0.113.0) and keeps only the first half of IPv6 addresses. Lockouts still use full addresses."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1287
msgid "<strong>Country</strong>: “Do not record”, or “Use the country Cloudflare provides”. In the free plugin a country is only recorded when the Visitor IP setting is “Through Cloudflare” (Cloudflare’s country header). It is never looked up through a third-party service."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1288
msgid "<strong>Lockout email</strong> (off by default): emails the site admin address when a lockout was triggered with an administrator’s username. At most one email per hour."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1289
msgid "<strong>New sign-in email</strong> (off by default): emails a user when their account signs in from a device (browser and system) or IP address it has not used before. Choose the roles under <strong>Who gets it</strong> (Administrator when first turned on). The first sign-in after it is turned on is only remembered. At most one email per account every 15 minutes. It runs after two-factor login, and links to the password reset. When Authlify Pro’s new-device alerts cover a user, Pro sends its own alert instead, so nobody gets two."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1290
msgid "<strong>Delete all entries</strong> empties the log at once. It cannot be undone."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1292
msgid "On multisite the log is shared by the network, so each site’s Activity screen shows entries from all sites, and clearing it clears every site."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1293
#, php-format
msgid "Privacy details are in %s."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1293
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1997
msgid "Retention, anonymization and personal data requests"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1297
msgid "Import, export and switching plugins"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1297
msgid "Move your setup between sites, copy settings from other login plugins, and decide what happens to your data on uninstall."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1299
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1654
msgid "Authlify → Settings"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1300
#: inc/Admin/ToolsPage.php:239
msgid "Switch from another plugin"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1300
msgid "Authlify lists the plugins whose settings it finds in your database, even if that plugin is no longer active. Click <strong>Import</strong> to copy them; nothing in the other plugin is changed."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1301
msgid "<strong>WPS Hide Login</strong>: the login address (applied immediately) and its redirect page."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1302
msgid "<strong>Limit Login Attempts Reloaded</strong>: allowed attempts, lockout length, retry window, the allow and block lists, and its username block list (as Refused usernames). Its username allow list is not copied. It also turns brute-force protection on."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1303
msgid "<strong>Admin and Site Enhancements</strong>: its custom login address (applied immediately)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1304
msgid "<strong>LoginPress</strong> and <strong>Colorlib Login Customizer</strong>: their login page design, into the Designer."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1305
msgid "WPS Hide Login and Limit Login Attempts Reloaded are also listed while they run on their default settings (nothing saved yet): Limit Login Attempts Reloaded’s defaults (4 attempts, 20 minutes) are imported; WPS Hide Login’s default address, /login, is one WordPress answers itself, so Authlify asks you to choose your own under Login URL instead."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1306
msgid "After importing a login address, deactivate the other plugin’s login feature at once: two plugins changing the login address can reveal it or lock you out."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1307
#: inc/Admin/ToolsPage.php:331
msgid "Two-factor secrets"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1307
msgid "When Two Factor, WP 2FA or Wordfence left authenticator-app data on this site, a <strong>Two-factor secrets</strong> panel appears. <strong>Preview</strong> counts who would be imported and changes nothing; <strong>Import</strong> copies the secrets, so people keep the same entry in their authenticator app. Then deactivate the other plugin: Authlify’s two-factor login only runs while no other two-factor plugin is active."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1308
msgid "<strong>Two Factor</strong>: users who have the authenticator app turned on there."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1309
msgid "<strong>WP 2FA</strong>: users whose method is the authenticator app. Its encrypted secrets are read with WP 2FA’s own key (the <code>WP2FA_ENCRYPT_KEY</code> constant in wp-config.php, or its stored key), so import before you remove WP 2FA’s data or that constant."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1310
msgid "Skipped and counted: users who already have an authenticator app in Authlify, apps saved but not turned on, and secrets that cannot be read."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1311
msgid "<strong>Wordfence Login Security</strong>, and the full Wordfence plugin that bundles it: every user with an authenticator app there, with their unused recovery codes unless they already have backup codes in Authlify. The code they last used there cannot be used again here."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1312
msgid "Backup codes from Two Factor and WP 2FA and any email codes are not copied."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1313
msgid "Each imported user and each import run is recorded in the activity log."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1315
msgid "Export"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1315
msgid "<strong>Download</strong> saves <code>authlify-settings-YYYY-MM-DD.json</code> with your settings and login page design. The CAPTCHA secret key and any unconfirmed login address change are left out. The file does contain the login address and the CAPTCHA site key, so store it privately."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: plugin name
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1316
#: inc/Admin/ToolsPage.php:257
#: inc/Admin/ToolsPage.php:289
#: inc/Admin/ToolsPage.php:351
msgid "Import"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1317
msgid "Choose a file exported by Authlify and click <strong>Import</strong>. Other files are refused."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1318
msgid "Only settings this site knows are imported. The CAPTCHA secret key, the uninstall choice and the setup status are never imported."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1319
msgid "The login address is only imported when you tick <strong>Also import the login URL</strong>. It then goes through the usual confirmation step."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1321
msgid "Data on uninstall"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1321
msgid "<strong>On uninstall</strong> (off by default): when on, deleting the plugin from the Plugins screen removes all Authlify settings, logs, lockout counters, two-factor data, passkeys and the compiled login page styles. Deactivating never deletes anything."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1326
msgid "WP-CLI commands"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1326
msgid "Recover the login address, clear lockouts, reset two-factor and run Leak Check from the command line."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1328
msgid "Login address"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1329
msgid "<code>get</code> prints the login address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1330
msgid "<code>set</code> applies a new address immediately (no confirmation step) and emails it to the site admin address. It refuses while <code>AUTHLIFY_SLUG</code> or <code>AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_HIDE</code> is defined."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1331
msgid "<code>reset</code> turns the custom address off, so wp-login.php works again."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1333
msgid "Lockouts"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1333
msgid "Unlocking an address also clears its network lockout."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1334
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2130
#: inc/TwoFactor/AdminPage.php:53
#: inc/TwoFactor/AdminPage.php:341
msgid "Two-factor"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1334
msgid "Removes a person’s two-factor methods so they can log in with their password and set it up again. Accepts a user ID, login name or email. Note the underscore in <code>reset_2fa</code>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1335
msgid "Runs Leak Check now and prints each result. It exits with an error when a leak is found, so you can use it in deployment scripts."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1360
msgid "What it gives you"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1361
msgid "Where it lives"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1375
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1647
#: inc/Admin/Upsell.php:115
msgid "Required two-factor for roles"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1375
msgid "Make two-factor login mandatory for the roles that matter, with a fair grace period to set it up."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1376
msgid "Choose the roles that must use two-factor, such as administrators, editors and shop managers."
