#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1662
msgid "Move from a closed or unmaintained plugin"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1662
msgid "Wordfence Login Security and the original Limit Login Attempts: what comes across, what does not, and when staying put is the better choice."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1663
#: inc/TwoFactor/Import.php:54
msgid "Wordfence Login Security"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1664
msgid "WordPress.org closed this standalone plugin in August 2026 and it no longer receives updates there. The login security in the full Wordfence plugin is still maintained. If you also want its firewall and scanner, staying on the full plugin is a sound choice and you do not need this guide."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1666
msgid "Back up your database. Keep the plugin installed and active until the import is done."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1667
#, php-format
msgid "Open %s. Under <strong>Two-factor secrets</strong>, click <strong>Preview</strong>, read the counts, then <strong>Import</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1667
msgid "Settings → Switch plugins"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1668
msgid "Deactivate Wordfence Login Security. Authlify’s two-factor step stays out of the way while it runs, so nothing changes for your users until this point."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1669
msgid "Log in with your own account in a private window, using your existing app entry. If you are stuck, <code>AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_2FA</code> in wp-config.php gets you back in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1670
msgid "Once you are happy, delete the plugin. Its copy of the secrets stays in its own database tables until then, and possibly after, depending on its settings."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1672
msgid "<strong>What comes across:</strong> authenticator apps and unused recovery codes. People keep the same app entry and do not enrol again."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1673
msgid "<strong>What does not:</strong> its settings, such as two-factor required by role, grace periods, remembered devices and its reCAPTCHA setup, and any passkeys registered in Wordfence 9. Requiring two-factor by role is an Authlify Pro feature; in the free plugin, two-factor stays each person’s choice, so someone who was only using it because it was required can switch it off. Passkeys have to be added again."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1674
msgid "The full Wordfence plugin keeps handling two-factor login while it is active, even after an import. Deactivating it also turns off its firewall and scanner, so decide that first."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1675
msgid "Limit Login Attempts (the original plugin)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1676
msgid "Its last update was in 2023. Under <strong>Switch from another plugin</strong>, <strong>Import</strong> copies its attempts, lockout length and retry window, or its defaults if its settings were never saved (4 attempts, 20 minutes, 12 hours). Its long lockout is replaced by Authlify’s escalating lockouts, which work differently. Its lockout history and log are not copied. It keeps no IP allow list of its own to copy. Deactivate it afterwards: two plugins counting the same failures lock people out sooner."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1680
msgid "Find your situation below; each has a way back in that needs no support ticket."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1681
msgid "Situation"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1681
msgid "Way back in"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1682
msgid "Search your email for “Your login address”, run <code>wp authlify url get</code>, or add <code>define( 'AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_HIDE', true );</code> to wp-config.php."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1683
msgid "“Too many failed login attempts”"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1683
msgid "Wait for the time shown, use “Email me an unlock link”, ask another administrator, or run <code>wp authlify unlock --all</code>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1684
msgid "“Access from your network is blocked”"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1684
msgid "Your address is on the block list. Another administrator can remove it on the Brute force tab. Otherwise, from the database or WP-CLI, empty the list: <code>wp eval \"\\Authlify\\Settings::update( array( 'ip_denylist' => '' ) );\"</code>"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1685
msgid "The CAPTCHA will not let you through"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1685
msgid "Add <code>define( 'AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_CAPTCHA', true );</code> to wp-config.php, log in, fix the keys or provider, then remove the line."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1686
msgid "Lost your phone and backup codes"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1686
msgid "Use the recovery email option on the two-factor screen if offered, ask another administrator to reset your two-factor, run <code>wp authlify reset_2fa you@example.com</code>, or add <code>define( 'AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_2FA', true );</code> to wp-config.php."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1687
msgid "The login address shows “page not found” right after a change"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1687
msgid "A page cache may be serving an old copy. Purge the cache, or use AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_HIDE while you exclude the address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1689
msgid "Remove any emergency line from wp-config.php as soon as you are back in; while it is there, that protection is off for everyone."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1693
msgid "Brand the login page"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1693
msgid "Make every login screen look like your site in a few minutes."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1696
msgid "Click <strong>Match my site</strong> to start from your theme’s colors, font and logo, or pick a template."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1697
msgid "Adjust the sections on the left (colors, layout, background, logo, form, fields, button, links, messages). The preview updates as you go."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1698
msgid "Use the screen switcher above the preview to check the other screens, such as lost password and two-factor."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1699
msgid "Save. Open your login address in a private window to see the result."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1700
#, php-format
msgid "Details: %s."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1704
msgid "Try a CAPTCHA in test mode first, then switch it on for real."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1706
#, php-format
msgid "Open %s and choose a provider. Cloudflare Turnstile suits most sites; ALTCHA needs no account and sends nothing elsewhere."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1706
msgid "Security → CAPTCHA"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1707
msgid "Paste the site key and secret key (not needed for ALTCHA), click <strong>Show preview</strong> and complete it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1708
msgid "Turn on <strong>Test mode</strong>, choose <strong>Only after failed logins</strong>, and save."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1709
msgid "After a few days, the Safety panel shows how many submissions would have been blocked in the last 7 days. Check the Activity log for “CAPTCHA failed” entries from real users."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1710
msgid "Turn test mode off."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1711
#, php-format
msgid "If a CAPTCHA ever stops you logging in, add %s to wp-config.php."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1731
msgid "I am locked out"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1731
msgid "“Too many failed login attempts”, a blocked address, or a second step you cannot pass."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1732
#, php-format
msgid "Start with %s, which lists every situation and its way back in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1734
msgid "Why did it happen?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1735
msgid "You (or someone sharing your internet connection) typed a wrong password too often. Offices, schools and mobile networks share addresses."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1736
msgid "Network lockouts are on and other addresses in your network failed too."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1737
msgid "Every visitor seems to come from one address because the site is behind a proxy or Cloudflare and the Visitor IP setting is wrong. Then a single attacker locks everyone out. See the proxy article."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1739
msgid "Prevent it next time"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1740
msgid "Add your fixed IP address to “Never lock out”."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1741
msgid "Use a password manager, and set up a passkey so you rarely type the password."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1742
msgid "Turn on the lockout email to hear about administrator lockouts."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1748
msgid "I lost the login address"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1748
msgid "Three ways to find or reset it without FTP."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1750
msgid "Search your email for “Your login address”. It is sent to the site admin address whenever the address changes."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1751
msgid "Run <code>wp authlify url get</code> if you have WP-CLI or SSH access."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1752
msgid "Add <code>define( 'AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_HIDE', true );</code> to wp-config.php, log in at <code>/wp-login.php</code>, check the address under Authlify → Login URL, then remove the line."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1753
msgid "If <code>AUTHLIFY_SLUG</code> is defined in wp-config.php, that value is the login address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1757
msgid "The new login address shows “page not found”"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1757
msgid "Usually an unconfirmed change, a cache, or a server rule."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1759
msgid "<strong>Not confirmed yet.</strong> A new address only works for 30 minutes before confirmation, and stops working if it expires. Open Authlify → Login URL (log in at the old address) and confirm or save it again."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1760
msgid "<strong>A cached copy.</strong> A page cache or CDN may have stored a “page not found” answer for that address before it existed. Purge the cache and exclude the address (Tools → Site Health lists the steps for the cache it finds)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1761
msgid "<strong>Plain permalinks.</strong> With plain permalinks the address is <code>https://example.com/?your-address</code>. The Login URL screen shows the exact form."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1762
msgid "<strong>Server rules.</strong> Some security rules on the web server (for example in .htaccess or nginx) block unknown paths or anything containing “login”. Authlify handles the address in WordPress itself, so the request must reach WordPress."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1763
msgid "<strong>Another plugin.</strong> A second “hide login” or security plugin may be changing the login address too. Authlify warns about the ones it knows on the Login URL screen."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1768
msgid "Cache conflicts"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1768
msgid "A cached login page breaks logins and can show one visitor’s page to another."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1771
msgid "The login page sends “do not store” headers and sets <code>DONOTCACHEPAGE</code>, which most caching plugins respect."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1772
msgid "WP Rocket, LiteSpeed Cache, SiteGround Optimizer and Breeze are told to skip the login address automatically, including an address waiting for confirmation."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1773
msgid "Tools → Site Health → “Exclude the Authlify login URL from your page cache” appears when it detects a cache that needs a manual exclusion (for example W3 Total Cache, WP Super Cache, WP Engine, Kinsta or Cloudflare APO) and lists the steps."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1775
msgid "Symptoms of a cached login page"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1776
msgid "“The link you followed has expired” or a failed login right after entering the right password."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1777
msgid "The login address shows “page not found” after you changed it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1778
msgid "CAPTCHA widgets that never load or always fail."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1780
msgid "Fix"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1780
msgid "Exclude <code>/your-address/</code> (and <code>/wp-login.php</code>) from every cache layer: the caching plugin, your host’s cache and the CDN. Then purge all caches."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1785
msgid "CAPTCHA not showing, or blocking people"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1785
msgid "Keys, caching, scripts and the reCAPTCHA v3 score."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1787
msgid "Not showing"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1788
msgid "Check the form is ticked under <strong>Forms</strong>, and whether <strong>When to show it</strong> is “Only after failed logins”: then login forms only show it after failures."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1789
msgid "Addresses on “Never lock out” never see a CAPTCHA."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1790
msgid "<code>AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_CAPTCHA</code> in wp-config.php turns it off; the CAPTCHA tab says so."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1791
msgid "A script optimiser that delays or combines JavaScript can stop the widget loading. Exclude the provider’s script and the login address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1792
msgid "WooCommerce forms only appear in the list when WooCommerce is active; the checkout option covers the classic checkout and the Checkout block."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1794
msgid "Blocking real people"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1795
msgid "Turn on <strong>Test mode</strong>: it logs failures without blocking while you investigate."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1796
msgid "For reCAPTCHA v3, lower the <strong>Minimum score</strong> (for example to 0.3)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1797
msgid "Make sure the site key is registered for this domain in the provider’s dashboard."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1798
msgid "Check the Activity log for “CAPTCHA failed” entries and their reason."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1800
msgid "Locked yourself out"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1800
msgid "Add <code>define( 'AUTHLIFY_DISABLE_CAPTCHA', true );</code> to wp-config.php, log in, fix the settings, and remove the line."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1805
msgid "Emails are not arriving"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1805
msgid "Login address, unlock and two-factor recovery emails use WordPress’s normal mail."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1807
msgid "Authlify sends email through <code>wp_mail()</code>, like the rest of WordPress. If password-reset emails do not arrive either, the problem is the site’s mail setup, not Authlify."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1808
msgid "Install an SMTP plugin, or use your host’s mail service, so mail is sent from an authenticated address. Check the spam folder."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1809
msgid "The login address email goes to the site admin address (Settings → General) and to the person who made the change."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1810
msgid "Unlock links go to the locked-out account’s own email address; at most three requests per hour per address are sent. Requests for unknown accounts are silently ignored."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1811
msgid "If mail is broken and you are locked out, use WP-CLI or the wp-config.php constants instead."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1816
msgid "Everyone gets locked out at once (proxies)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1816
msgid "Behind Cloudflare, a load balancer or a host’s proxy, every visitor can look like the same address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1818
msgid "Open Security → Brute force and look at <strong>Detected setup</strong> and <strong>Your IP address as seen now</strong>. If that address is not your own public IP (for example it is 10.x, 172.16–31.x, 192.168.x, or a Cloudflare address), the setting is wrong."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1819
msgid "Choose <strong>Through Cloudflare</strong> for Cloudflare, or <strong>Through my own proxy</strong> and list the proxy addresses under <strong>Trusted proxies</strong> (your host can tell you them)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1820
msgid "Click <strong>Unlock everyone</strong> afterwards, or run <code>wp authlify unlock --all</code>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1821
msgid "Tools → Site Health warns when the setting does not match the requests it sees."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1822
msgid "Never trust forwarding headers from everyone: attackers could then choose any address, including one on your “Never lock out” list. That is why Authlify only reads them from proxies you name."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1826
msgid "WooCommerce"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1826
msgid "How Authlify works with the My Account login, checkout and registration."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1828
msgid "The WooCommerce My Account login form keeps working and is not hidden. Brute-force lockouts apply to it, and the Activity log marks its logins with the “woocommerce” channel."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1829
msgid "CAPTCHA can protect the WooCommerce login, registration, lost password and checkout forms. The checkout switch covers guest orders on both the classic checkout and the Checkout block."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1830
msgid "The breached-password check also runs when customers register, change their password in My Account, or create an account at checkout."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1831
msgid "With Force login on, add your shop, cart, checkout and My Account paths to Public pages if customers must reach them."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1832
msgid "Customers never need wp-admin, so hiding /wp-admin/ does not affect them."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1837
msgid "What changes when Authlify runs on a network."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1839
msgid "Network-activate it to manage one set of settings for all sites from Network Admin → Authlify. Settings start from the main site’s settings. Only super admins can change them."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1840
msgid "The activity log, lockout counters and passkeys are stored in shared tables, so lockouts apply across the network and each site’s Activity screen shows entries from all sites."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1841
msgid "wp-signup.php and wp-activate.php are not hidden, because the network needs them."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1842
msgid "“Application passwords: Administrators only” means super admins on a network."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1843
msgid "The uninstall option is read from the network settings; when the plugin is activated per site rather than network-wide, deleting it does not remove data."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1848
msgid "Passkeys are not offered"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1848
msgid "Passkeys need PHP 8, https, and a browser or device that supports them."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1850
msgid "<strong>PHP 8.0 or newer.</strong> On older PHP, the passkey option is greyed out on the Two-factor screen with the PHP version shown. Ask your host to upgrade."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1851
msgid "<strong>https.</strong> Browsers only allow passkeys on secure pages (https, or localhost while developing)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1852
msgid "<strong>The same address.</strong> A passkey belongs to the site’s domain. After moving the site to a new domain, existing passkeys stop working and must be added again."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1853
msgid "<strong>The method is offered.</strong> “Passkeys and security keys” must be ticked under Offered methods on the Two-factor screen."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1854
msgid "<strong>The login button</strong> only appears once at least one person has added a passkey, and when “Passkey sign-in” is on."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1859
msgid "Conflicts with other security plugins"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1859
msgid "Which features overlap, and what to switch off."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1861
msgid "<strong>Hide login plugins</strong> (WPS Hide Login, WP Ghost, and similar) and the “change login URL” feature of security suites: run only one. Authlify names the ones it detects on the Login URL screen. Import the address, then switch the other off."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1862
msgid "<strong>Two-factor plugins.</strong> When another two-factor plugin is active, Authlify steps aside: it adds no second step or passkey button, and the Two-factor screen says which plugin is in charge."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1863
msgid "<strong>Limit-login plugins and security suites</strong> (Wordfence, Solid Security, All In One WP Security and others) can run alongside, but two sets of lockouts and two CAPTCHAs confuse people. Keep one of each."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1864
msgid "<strong>Login customizers</strong> such as LoginPress: import the design into Authlify’s Designer, then deactivate them."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1865
msgid "<strong>Server firewalls</strong> (mod_security, host rules) may block the login address or CAPTCHA requests. Check the host’s logs if a request never reaches WordPress."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1886
msgid "What is the difference between Free and Pro?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1886
msgid "Everything described under “Free features” is free for good. Pro adds tools for teams, stores and agencies."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1887
msgid "The free plugin includes the private login address, Leak Check, brute-force lockouts, all CAPTCHA providers and the honeypot, two-factor login with authenticator apps, backup codes and passkeys, the breached-password check, hardening, redirects, the activity log, the login page designer, import and export, and WP-CLI. There are no usage limits and no license."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1888
#: inc/Admin/Upsell.php:122
msgid "Authlify Pro adds:"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1890
msgid "Two-factor rules: required two-factor for chosen roles with a grace period, email codes, trusted devices, sudo mode and a coverage report."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1891
msgid "Passwordless sign-in: login links, email sign-in codes and temporary access links."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1892
msgid "Social and single sign-on: Google, Microsoft, Apple, GitHub and any OpenID Connect provider."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1893
msgid "Alerts and monitoring: new-device and new-country alerts, Slack, Discord, Telegram and webhooks, a local country database."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1894
msgid "Sessions, password policy and access rules by country, time or IP."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1895
msgid "Premium templates, branded emails, login blocks, a popup login and a WooCommerce My Account skin."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1896
msgid "Agency tools: network-wide settings with locks, white-label, client handoff and design sync, plus a REST API."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1897
#, php-format
msgid "Each is described under %s."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1901
msgid "Is it compatible with my site?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1901
msgid "Requirements, and how it works with common plugins and hosts."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1903
msgid "WordPress 6.4 or newer and PHP 7.4 or newer. Passkeys need PHP 8.0."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1904
msgid "Single sites and multisite networks."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1905
msgid "WooCommerce: lockouts, CAPTCHA, the two-factor step and the breached-password check cover its forms. In the free plugin, people who cannot open wp-admin, such as customers, cannot set up two-factor themselves: WooCommerce sends them to My Account, which has no two-factor section. Setting it up inside My Account is an Authlify Pro feature. Someone already enrolled gets the step on these forms either way."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1906
msgid "Caching plugins: WP Rocket, LiteSpeed Cache, SiteGround Optimizer and Breeze are configured automatically; Site Health gives steps for others."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1907
msgid "Cloudflare and reverse proxies: supported through the Visitor IP setting."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1908
msgid "Other two-factor plugins: Authlify steps aside when Two Factor, WP 2FA or Wordfence Login Security is active."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1909
msgid "Other “hide login” plugins: run only one; Authlify can import their address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1914
msgid "Does Authlify send my data anywhere?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1914
msgid "No, unless you switch on a feature that needs a service, and then only what that feature needs."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1915
msgid "The activity log, lockouts and two-factor data stay in your database. Authlify contacts another service only for a CAPTCHA provider you choose, and for the breached-password check if you turn it on. It loads no fonts, scripts or images from a CDN on its own."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1916
#, php-format
msgid "The full list is in %s."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1916
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2009
msgid "External services"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1920
msgid "Is hiding the login page real security?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1920
msgid "It removes the noise; lockouts, CAPTCHA and two-factor provide the protection."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1921
msgid "A private login address stops the automated scripts that only know wp-login.php, which is most of them, and Leak Check proves it is not revealed. But an address is not a password: anyone who learns it can try to log in. That is why Authlify also limits attempts, supports CAPTCHA, checks for breached passwords and offers two-factor login and passkeys. Use them together."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1925
msgid "How do updates work?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1925
msgid "Through WordPress.org, like any free plugin."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1927
msgid "Updates appear under Dashboard → Updates and on the Plugins screen, and work with automatic updates."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1928
msgid "Settings, the log and two-factor data are kept; any database changes run on their own after the update."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1929
msgid "Authlify Pro is updated separately from its own update server while its license is active. Its features keep working when a license lapses."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1934
msgid "I used Modify Login. What changed?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1934
msgid "Modify Login is now Authlify. Your login address, redirects and log carry over."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1936
msgid "The login address, the hiding and redirect behavior, login and logout redirects, activity logging and reCAPTCHA v2 keys (on the login form) carry over. The newest 50,000 log entries are copied; the old log table is kept."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1937
msgid "Your old login page design is kept as a design in the Designer."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1938
msgid "New protection (brute-force lockouts, two-factor login, the weekly Leak Check, the honeypot, the other CAPTCHA forms) stays off until you switch it on. The notice “Modify Login is now Authlify” has a <strong>Review protection</strong> button that opens Security."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1939
msgid "If the old address had characters that are no longer allowed, the notice shows the new address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1940
msgid "Old bookmarks to the Modify Login screens still open the right Authlify screens."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1945
msgid "What happens when I deactivate or delete it?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1945
msgid "Deactivating keeps everything. Deleting removes data only if you asked it to."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1947
msgid "<strong>Deactivate</strong>: the login page goes back to wp-login.php at once, lockouts and two-factor stop, and all settings and data are kept for when you reactivate."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1948
msgid "<strong>Delete</strong>: by default, settings and data are kept so a reinstall picks up where you left off."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1949
msgid "To remove everything, turn on <strong>Authlify → Settings → Data → On uninstall</strong> before deleting. Deleting then removes the three Authlify tables, every Authlify option and user meta (including two-factor secrets and passkeys), the compiled login styles and the scheduled tasks. Old Modify Login options and log are removed too."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1950
msgid "<strong>Multisite</strong>: when Authlify was activated site by site, each site's own \"On uninstall\" choice applies. Sites that asked are cleaned (settings, design files, scheduled tasks and their log entries); the shared tables stay while any site keeps its data. When it was network-activated, the network setting decides for every site."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1955
msgid "Can I go back to Modify Login 2.x?"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1955
msgid "Yes, nothing is lost, but the old version only knows its own settings."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1957
msgid "Installing Modify Login 2.x over Authlify works and deletes nothing. All Authlify settings, logs, two-factor data and designs stay in the database."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1958
msgid "2.x reads its own old settings, so the <strong>login URL goes back to the one you had in 2.x</strong>. A login URL you chose in Authlify stops working (it shows \"page not found\") until you update again. Note your 2.x login address before you roll back."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1959
msgid "Everything 2.x does not have stops while it is active: lockouts, two-factor login, passkeys, the honeypot and CAPTCHA providers other than reCAPTCHA."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1960
msgid "Updating to Authlify again picks up exactly where you left off. Changes you made in 2.x in the meantime are not copied over again."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1961
msgid "If you are locked out after a rollback, the 2.x login address or <code>wp-login.php</code> (if 2.x was not hiding it) gets you in."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1982
msgid "What Authlify stores"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1982
msgid "Every table, option and user field the free plugin writes."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1984
msgid "Database tables"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1985
msgid "The activity log: time (UTC), event, user ID, username, IP address (shortened if you anonymize), two-letter country when known, browser (user agent) and details such as the login channel or failure reason."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1986
msgid "Lockout counters: the IP address, network or account, the number of recent failures, the time of the last failure and any lockout end time. Rows older than a day without a lockout are removed daily."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1987
msgid "Registered passkeys: user ID, site domain, credential ID, public key, signature counter, the name the person gave it, and when it was added and last used. Removed when the user is deleted."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1988
msgid "On multisite these tables are shared by the network."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1989
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1989
msgid "<code>authlify_settings</code> (all settings; a network option when network-activated), <code>authlify_design</code> (the login page design), <code>authlify_version</code>, <code>authlify_db_version</code>, <code>authlify_leak_check</code> (the last result), <code>authlify_log_error</code> (only if writing to the log failed), and on upgraded sites <code>authlify_migrated_from</code> and related markers. Short-lived transients hold rate limits, unlock and recovery tokens, and drafts."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1990
msgid "User meta"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1990
msgid "Only for people who use two-factor or dismiss a notice: <code>authlify_2fa</code> (on/off flag), <code>authlify_totp_secret</code> (encrypted), <code>authlify_totp_pending</code>, <code>authlify_totp_last_step</code>, <code>authlify_backup_codes</code> (hashed), <code>authlify_backup_codes_created</code>, <code>authlify_passkey_handle</code>, <code>authlify_passkey_register</code>, <code>authlify_2fa_login</code> (a hashed, 10-minute sign-in token), <code>authlify_2fa_recovery</code> (a hashed recovery token), <code>authlify_2fa_coexist_dismissed</code> and <code>authlify_seen_welcome</code>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1991
msgid "Files"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1991
msgid "<code>wp-content/uploads/authlify/</code> holds the compiled login page CSS."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1992
msgid "What is not stored"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1992
msgid "Passwords are never stored or logged by Authlify, including failed ones. The CAPTCHA answers and the breached-password lookups are not stored either (only the public breach list for a hash prefix is cached for a day)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1997
msgid "How long the log is kept, how to shorten IP addresses, and how WordPress’s privacy tools cover Authlify."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1999
#, php-format
msgid "<strong>Retention.</strong> Log entries are deleted after 90 days by default (%s → Keep entries for). 0 keeps them forever. Deletion runs daily."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1999
msgid "Activity → Settings"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2000
msgid "<strong>Anonymize IPs.</strong> Stores IPv4 addresses with the last part set to 0 and keeps only the first half of IPv6 addresses. It applies to new entries. Lockouts still use full addresses, briefly, in the lockout counters."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2001
msgid "<strong>Turn logging off</strong> with the Activity log switch; lockouts keep working. Login address changes are always recorded."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2002
msgid "<strong>Export and erase.</strong> Authlify registers “Authlify login activity” with WordPress’s Export Personal Data and Erase Personal Data tools (Tools menu). They cover log entries linked to the person’s user account; failed attempts made with a username that does not exist are not linked to anyone."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2003
msgid "<strong>Privacy policy.</strong> Authlify adds suggested text to Settings → Privacy → Policy guide."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2004
msgid "<strong>Delete everything.</strong> See “What happens when I deactivate or delete it?”."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2009
msgid "Which outside services can be contacted, when, and what they receive. Nothing is contacted unless you turn on the feature that needs it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2011
msgid "Free plugin"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2012
msgid "CAPTCHA provider (only the one you choose)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2012
msgid "Cloudflare Turnstile (challenges.cloudflare.com), hCaptcha (js.hcaptcha.com, api.hcaptcha.com) or Google reCAPTCHA (www.google.com). The visitor’s browser loads the widget on protected forms; your server sends the answer, your secret key and the visitor’s IP address to the provider to check it. ALTCHA contacts nobody. Nothing is contacted while the provider is “None”."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2013
msgid "Have I Been Pwned (only with the breached-password check on)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2013
msgid "api.pwnedpasswords.com receives the first 5 characters of the SHA-1 hash of a new password, never the password."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2014
msgid "Your own site (Leak Check)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2014
msgid "Leak Check sends requests only to your own site’s address, while a custom login address is set."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2015
msgid "The free plugin loads no fonts, scripts, images or avatars from other services on its own, and sends no usage data. Emails are sent through your site’s own mail setup."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2016
msgid "Authlify Pro (when installed)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2017
msgid "License and update server"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2017
msgid "store.mantrabrain.com. With a license key saved: when you activate, refresh or deactivate it, and in a background update check about every 12 hours. Without a key, only when you click “Check for updates” on the License screen or “View details” on the Plugins screen. It sends the license key (if saved), the site address, the Pro version and the environment type (production, staging and so on)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2018
msgid "DB-IP (only if you choose the local country database)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:2018
